<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Tolentino</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-first-coalition">War of the First Coalition</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Tolentino was signed on 19 February 1797 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vi">Pope Pius VI</a> and the French Republic, represented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>. The Papal States gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> and the Comtat Venaissin, ceded the Legations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>, left Ancona under French occupation and paid a heavy indemnity, including works of art sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. It began the dismantling of the pope's temporal power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tolentino.jpg" alt="Treaty of Tolentino" width="300" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trait%C3%A9_de_Tolentino_(1797).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>'s Italian campaign of 1796 and 1797 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-first-coalition">War of the First Coalition</a>. After defeating the Austrians at the Battle of Arcole, the Battle of Rivoli and at Mantua, Bonaparte had no enemies left in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and could turn against the Papal States. Nine months of negotiations had produced no settlement, and in February 1797 some 9,000 French soldiers invaded the papal region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vi">Pope Pius VI</a> no choice but to accept French terms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was represented by Bonaparte and by François Cacault, the Directory's ambassador to the Holy See, facing four representatives of the papal curia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty confirmed the harsh terms of the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> and added 15 million livres to the 21 million already taken from the Papal States, 36 million in all. The cession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> and the Comtat Venaissin, occupied by French troops early in the Revolution, ended about half a millennium of papal rule there. The legations, already in French hands, were incorporated into the newly created Cispadane Republic.</p><p>More than a hundred paintings and other works of art were to go to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and the French commissioners could enter any public, religious or private building to choose what would be taken. In 1798 treaties with other Italian states extended this practice to all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The pope also had to compensate the family of the French journalist Hugh de Basseville, who had been killed by a crowd in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for allegedly insulting the pope.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The removal of the art was criticised by many as theft and plunder. Among its fiercest opponents was Quatremère de Quincy, who had argued in a pamphlet of 1796 that taking a work from the place for which it was made irreparably impaired its meaning. Most of the works were returned after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s fall. The treaty did not satisfy the Directory, which still sought to end the temporal power of the Holy See: a year later the French army invaded the rest of the Papal States, arrested Pius VI and set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Tolentino” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…annexed Avignon and the Comtat Venaissin in 1791, Napoleon forced Pius VI to cede the Legations of Bologna, Ferrara and Romagna at the <b>Treaty of Tolentino</b> in 1797, and in 1798 French troops proclaimed a Roman Republic and carried the pope into exile, where he died in 1799.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>French troops occupied Ancona, the main Adriatic port of the Papal States, in February 1797, and the <b>Treaty of Tolentino</b> left them in control of it.</blockquote><a href="/state/anconine-republic">Anconine Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Revolution, after local votes, the National Constituent Assembly annexed Avignon and the Comtat to France in 1791; the papacy accepted the loss in the <b>Treaty of Tolentino</b> in 1797.</blockquote><a href="/state/comtat-venaissin">Comtat Venaissin</a></li></ul></section>
